WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.960
In the same way that Kylo Ren's ultra-high-waisted
pants will never catch on as the next hot

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.030
fashion trend, it's an undeniable fact that
not everyone loved Star Wars: The Last Jedi.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:15.040
When the film launched in theaters in December
2017, audiences were more divided than Darth

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:15.540
Maul.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.500
Now, as we're nearing the one-year anniversary
of the Rian Johnson-directed flick, an old

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.680
complaint about The Last Jedi has resurfaced.

00:00:23.169 --> 00:00:27.540
But it's not just any old gripe - it's one
that regards a plot hole that one YouTuber

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.740
claims, quote, "broke Star Wars forever."

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:34.660
The scene in question is the one in which
Vice Admiral Amilyn Holdo does something no

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.790
character has ever even attempted to do in
the history of Star Wars: she kicks General

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:43.580
Leia Organa's ship into lightspeed and proceeds
to drive it directly into Supreme Leader Snoke's

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:48.780
ship, the Mega-class Star Dreadnought that
served as the First Order's mobile headquarters.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.600
Holdo's move allowed the Rebel Alliance members
aboard the Raddus a narrow window of time

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.200
to escape on the lifeboats before the First
Order could track the Raddus through hyperspace.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.540
Before this moment in The Last Jedi, fans
had never seen a Star Wars character carry

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.120
out a kamikaze attack.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.640
Once they did, well, it got the gears in their
brains turning.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.710
Some franchise loyals, like YouTuber Thor
Skywalker, feel that the scene ripped a hole

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:17.420
in more than than the Supremacy - it opened
up a plot hole in the entire Star Wars universe.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:22.180
Thor Skywalker argues that if Holdo could
damage the Supremacy by jumping to lightspeed

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:26.370
and crashing into it, the Rebels could have
done the exact same thing to the Death Star

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.160
in A New Hope.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.980
He further claims that the so-called "Holdo
Maneuver" renders the Rebel Alliance's attack

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.750
on the Death Star somewhat ineffective, since
they could have easily created a battle station

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.640
that could implement the same tactic Holdo
used, one that also has, quote, "the ability

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.500
to send or launch massive objects into hyperspace
at planets or whatever else it might want

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.060
to destroy."

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.820
In his argument, he stated:

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.600
"Why doesn't Holdo just put some droids on
some transport ships and have them jumping

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.700
into hyperspace at the First Order fleet?

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.000
She apparently knew such an action would work
so it doesn't make sense why she waited so

00:01:59.000 --> 00:01:59.920
long to employ it.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.480
You can then go back to any battle in Star
Wars history and wonder why the Holdo Maneuver

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:05.480
wasn't used.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.260
[...] [The Last Jedi] seems to be the most
reckless in terms of what it lets into the

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:09.760
canon.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:15.180
It pulled apart, or broke, the rest of Star
Wars by introducing things that makes us question

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.420
everything that comes before it."

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.260
We can certainly see where these arguments
are coming from.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.879
In A New Hope, the Rebels probably could have
done what Holdo did in The Last Jedi.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.380
But would it have made the movie better?

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.060
Nope!

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.280
"Then man your ships, and may the force be
with you.”

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.620
“Yay!”

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.139
While Holdo's attack does signify a bit of
a logic question in the Star Wars universe,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.860
to call it a franchise-breaking plot hole
is a little much.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.359
To some fans, the "Holdo Maneuver" truly has
put an irreversible dent in the series.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.500
To others, it's not that big of a deal.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.180
As far as director Rian Johnson is concerned,
the moment was actually inspired by the original

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:57.760
trilogy.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.290
He explained to the Empire Film Podcast:

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:05.120
"Ever since Han is talking about how if you
don't get the calculations right you could

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.360
go through, you know, I've always wanted to
see what that would look like."

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.269
Conflicting opinions about whether or not
The Last Jedi broke Star Wars aside, writer-director

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:17.819
J.J. Abrams could still keep the franchise
afloat with Episode IX, set for release on

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.620
December 20, 2019.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:20.940
So what do you think?

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.540
Did the Holdo maneuver "break" Star Wars for
you?

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.640
Let us know in the comments!

